eeds at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the lower\u2011bound proof shows that fewer tiles are impossible. Therefore the minimum number of tiles is \\(2n-2 = 4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nWe work with the \\(n\\times n\\) grid of unit squares whose rows are indexed \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) from top to bottom and columns \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) from left to right.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Reformulation\n\nBecause each row and each column must contain **exactly one** uncovered unit square, the set \\(U\\) of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a bijection  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\qquad\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other unit squares must be covered by axis\u2011parallel rectangles (tiles) that are pairwise interior\u2011disjoint.\n\nThus the problem asks for the smallest possible number of rectangles that partition  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl([n]\\times[n]\\bigr)\\setminus U .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A construction with \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nChoose the permutation \\(p(i)=i\\); i.e. the uncovered squares are the main diagonal\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nDenote by  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(r,c)\\mid c<r\\}\\qquad\\text{(the lower\u2011triangular part)},\n\\]\n\\[\nR=\\{(r,c)\\mid c>r\\}\\qquad\\text{(the upper\u2011triangular part)} .\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) are disjoint and together constitute the set of squares to be tiled.\n\n*For the lower\u2011triangular part* \\(L\\): for each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_j=\\{(r,c)\\mid c=j,\\; r=j+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_j\\) is a rectangle of size \\((n-j)\\times1\\).  \nThe family \\(\\{T_j\\mid j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) pairwise disjoint rectangles whose union is exactly \\(L\\).\n\n*For the upper\u2011triangular part* \\(R\\): for each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i=\\{(r,c)\\mid r=i,\\; c=i+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(S_i\\) is a rectangle of size \\(1\\times (n-i)\\).  \nThe family \\(\\{S_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) pairwise disjoint rectangles whose union is exactly \\(R\\).\n\nThus the whole complement \\(([n]\\times[n])\\setminus U\\) is tiled by  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\nrectangles.  \nFor \\(n=2025\\) this yields \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Lower bound: at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary\n\nWe prove that **any** tiling of \\(([n]\\times[n])\\setminus U\\) requires at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n#### 3.1  Ordering the rows\n\nRe\u2011index the rows so that the uncovered columns appear in increasing order:\nlet \\(\\sigma\\) be a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\np(\\sigma(1))<p(\\sigma(2))<\\dots <p(\\sigma(n)).\n\\]\n\nRename the rows by \\(i\\mapsto\\sigma(i)\\); the set of uncovered squares becomes  \n\n\\[\nU'=\\{(i,q(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\qquad\nq(1)<q(2)<\\dots <q(n),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(q(i)=p(\\sigma(i))\\).  \nSince \\(q\\) is a permutation, the numbers \\(q(i)-1\\) are exactly the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\) in some order.\n\n#### 3.2  The left side (cells with column\u202f<\u202funcovered column)\n\nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nL'=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<q(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nIn row \\(i\\) the left side contains precisely \\(q(i)-1\\) cells.  \nBecause the sequence \\(q(i)-1\\) runs through all values \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\), the shape of \\(L'\\) is the *staircase* (Ferrers diagram) of partition \\((n-1,n-2,\\dots ,0)\\).\n\nA rectangle that lies inside \\(L'\\) cannot contain two cells from different columns, for otherwise it would have to cross the uncovered cell in the column having the smaller \\(q\\)-value. Consequently each rectangle covering part of \\(L'\\) is confined to a single column.  \n\nSince column \\(j\\) (for \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\)) contains at least one cell of \\(L'\\) (namely the cells in rows \\(i\\) with \\(q(i)>j\\)), we need **at least one** rectangle for each of those \\(n-1\\) columns. Hence any tiling of \\(L'\\) uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n#### 3.3  The right side (cells with column\u202f>\u202funcovered column)\n\nAn identical argument applied to  \n\n\\[\nR'=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>q(i)\\}\n\\]\n\n(the cells to the right of the uncovered squares) shows that at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to tile \\(R'\\).\n\n#### 3.4  Combining the bounds\n\nSince the two families \\(L'\\) and \\(R'\\) are disjoint, the total number of rectangles needed to tile the whole complement is at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Minimality\n\nThe construction of Section\u202f2 uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles, meeting the lower bound proved in Section\u202f3.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is precisely  \n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer:** The smallest number of rectangular tiles that can be placed under the given conditions is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
