s another \\(n-1\\) rectangles.  \n   All cells except the diagonal are covered, no rectangle contains a diagonal cell, and the rectangles are pairwise disjoint. Hence the board can be tiled with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n4. Since the lower bound equals the construction, the minimum number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048 .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet the board be an \\(n\\times n\\) grid with \\(n=2025\\).  \nIn each row and each column exactly one unit square is left uncovered.  \nConsequently the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there is a permutation\n\\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are precisely\n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other squares must be covered by axis‑parallel rectangular tiles whose interiors are\npairwise disjoint.\n\nFor a fixed row \\(i\\) denote  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad   \nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid \\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus the set of covered squares is the disjoint union  \n\n\\[\nC=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i\\;\\cup\\;\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i .\n\\]\n\nA tile lying completely inside the left part must be a rectangle whose columns are\ncontained in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,\\sigma(i)-1\\}\\) for every row \\(i\\) it occupies; similarly a tile\nin the right part uses only columns \\(\\{\\sigma(i)+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A lower bound for the left part\n\nFor each column \\(c\\;(1\\le c\\le n-1)\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_c=\\{\\,i\\mid \\sigma(i)>c\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nExactly the rows in \\(S_c\\) contain the cell \\((i,c)\\) in their left part \\(L_i\\).\nThe set \\(S_c\\) is a subset of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); write it as a union of maximal\nconsecutive intervals\n\n\\[\nS_c = I_{c,1}\\cup I_{c,2}\\cup\\cdots\\cup I_{