left region covered by vertical rectangles each covering a column below diagonal, right region covered by horizontal rectangles each covering a row to the right of diagonal. That uses exactly n-1 vertical rectangles and n-1 horizontal rectangles, total 2n-2.\n\nIs there any possibility to use fewer rectangles by mixing left and right region across rows? No, they claim a tile cannot contain cells from both left and right region of the same row because uncovered square lies between them. That's correct: any rectangle covering a cell left of uncovered square and a cell right of uncovered square in same row would also cover the uncovered square (since rectangle includes all columns between leftmost and rightmost cells in that row). So each rectangle is either entirely left or entirely right of the uncovered squares for each row. However, a rectangle could possibly include cells from left region of some rows and right region of other rows? Let's think: Suppose a rectangle spans multiple rows. For each row it covers, the rectangle's columns are a contiguous interval. If the rectangle includes some column left of the uncovered square for a row, and also includes some column right of the uncovered square for a different row, then the rectangle's column interval must include the uncovered column for at least one of those rows (maybe not). Actually, consider rectangle spanning rows i and j. For row i, uncovered column is sigma(i). For row j, uncovered column is sigma(j). The rectangle's column interval is [c1, c2]. If for row i, all covered cells are left of sigma(i), then c2 < sigma(i). If for row j, all covered cells are right of sigma(j), then c1 > sigma(j). So we need c2 < sigma(i) and c1 > sigma(j). This would require sigma(j) < c1 <= c2 < sigma(i). So sigma(j) < sigma(i). So it's possible if sigma(j) < sigma(i). But then the rectangle's column interval is between sigma(j) and sigma(i). However, does that interval contain any uncovered squares? It includes column sigma(j) for row j? Actually column sigma(j) is the uncovered column for row j, but rectangle's column interval is [c1, c2] with c1 > sigma(j). So column sigma(j) is not included. Similarly, column sigma(i) is not included because c2 < sigma(i). So the rectangle does not contain uncovered squares. So it's possible to have a rectangle that covers left region of some rows and right region of other rows, as long as the column interval lies strictly between the uncovered columns of those rows. But the solution's claim that \"A tile cannot contain a cell from the left region of a certain row and a cell from the right region of the same row\" is true, but they also claim \"Hence every tile belongs wholly to the left region or wholly to the right region.\" This is a stronger claim: they claim a tile cannot mix left region of some rows and right region of other rows. Let's examine that more carefully.\n\nWe need to see if a rectangle can contain cells from left region of some rows and right region of other rows. The rectangle is defined by a set of rows [r1, r2] and columns [c1, c2]. For each row i in [r1, r2], the rectangle covers cells (i, j) for j in [c1, c2]. For a given row i, the uncovered column sigma(i) splits the row into left region (j < sigma(i)) and right region (j > sigma(i)). The rectangle's column interval [c1, c2] may intersect left region, right region, or both. If [c1, c2] lies entirely left of sigma(i) (i.e., c2 < sigma(i)), then rectangle covers only left region cells for row i. If [c1, c2] lies entirely right of sigma(i) (c1 > sigma(i)), then rectangle covers only right region cells for row i. If [c1, c2] straddles sigma(i) (c1 <= sigma(i) <= c2), then rectangle includes the uncovered cell (i, sigma(i)), which is not allowed. So for each row i, the rectangle must either be wholly left or wholly right of sigma(i). But the rectangle's column interval is the same for all rows. So for rows i and j, we need either both have sigma(i) > c2 (so rectangle left of both) or both have sigma(i) < c1 (rectangle right of both), or one has sigma(i) > c2 (left) and the other has sigma(j) < c1 (right). In the latter case, we require sigma(j) < c1 <= c2 < sigma(i). So sigma(j) < sigma(i). So it's possible if the uncovered columns are ordered such that sigma(j) < sigma(i). So a rectangle can be left of sigma(i) and right of sigma(j) simultaneously. This would mean the rectangle's column interval lies between sigma(j) and sigma(i). For rows where sigma is between c1 and c2, the rectangle would be invalid. So we need that for all rows in the rectangle's row interval, sigma(i) is either all > c2 or all < c1, or sigma(i) are either all > c2 or all < c1? Actually, if we have some rows where sigma(i) > c2 (left region) and some rows where sigma(i) < c1 (right region), then the rectangle's column interval must lie between the smallest sigma among left rows and the largest sigma among right rows? Let's examine: Suppose we have rows i in set A where sigma(i) > c2, and rows j in set B where sigma(j) < c1. For rows i in A, rectangle is left region; for rows j in B, rectangle is right region. For a rectangle to be valid, we need c2 < sigma(i) for all i in A, and c1 > sigma(j) for all j in B. So we need c2 < min_{i in A} sigma(i) and c1 > max_{j in B} sigma(j). So we need max_{j in B} sigma(j) < c1 <= c2 < min_{i in A} sigma(i). So the column interval [c1, c2] lies strictly between the sigma values of the two groups. So if the permutation sigma has some rows with sigma values less than some others, we can pick a rectangle that covers rows where sigma values are either all above or all below the interval. For that rectangle to be valid, the sigma values for rows in the rectangle must not intermix: they must be either all above the interval or all below it. But if we have rows with sigma values both above and below the interval, then the rectangle would be invalid for those rows because they'd have sigma(i) in between c1 and c2. So the rectangle can only include rows whose sigma values are either all > c2 (i.e., all sigma values are larger than c2) or all < c1 (i.e., all sigma values are smaller than c1). But can we have rows with sigma values both > c2 and < c1? That would require sigma values on both sides of the interval, but the rectangle includes both rows. However, the condition we derived above (max sigma in B < c1 <= c2 < min sigma in A) ensures that sigma values for rows in B are less than c1 and sigma values for rows in A are greater than c2. So indeed we can have rows with sigma values on both sides of the interval, as long as the interval lies between them. So the rectangle can include rows from B (right region) and rows from A (left region). So the claim that \"every tile belongs wholly to the left region or wholly to the right region\" is false: a tile could be left of some uncovered squares and right of others, as long as its column interval lies between the sigma values of those rows.\n\nBut is that allowed given the condition that each row has exactly one uncovered square? Yes. So the solution's claim that each tile must be wholly in left region or wholly in right region is not correct. However, does that affect the lower bound? The lower bound argument used that each rectangle can cover at most one interval per column in left region and similarly for right region. But if a rectangle can cover left region cells in some rows and right region cells in other rows, then it might be able to cover intervals in both left and right region simultaneously, potentially reducing the total number of rectangles needed.\n\nThe solution's lower bound argument counts intervals in left region across columns and intervals in right region across columns, and then says each rectangle can contribute at most one interval to a given column. But a rectangle could contribute intervals to both left and right region across columns? Let's examine: For a given column c, the rectangle covers that column for some rows. If those rows have sigma(i) > c (i.e., uncovered squares to the right of column c), then the rectangle covers left region cells in that column. If those rows have sigma(i) < c, then rectangle covers right region cells in that column. If the rectangle includes rows from both groups, then for column c, the rectangle would cover cells both left and right of the uncovered squares for those rows. But if sigma(i) > c for some rows and sigma(j) < c for other rows, then for those rows the rectangle would be left region for some rows and right region for others. However, for column c, the rectangle's rows are contiguous (since rectangle's rows are a contiguous interval). If sigma values are monotonic across rows (i.e., permutation sigma is not monotonic), then it's possible that within a contiguous block of rows, sigma values can be both above and below c. In that case, the rectangle covering column c would include some rows where c is left of sigma(i) and some rows where c is right of sigma(j). But for those rows where sigma(i) > c, the rectangle's column interval includes column c which is left of sigma(i), okay. For rows where sigma(j) < c, column c is right of sigma(j), okay. So rectangle can cover column c for both left and right region cells. However, does that cause any uncovered squares to be covered? No, because column c is not equal to sigma(i) for any row i in the rectangle (since sigma(i) != c for all i). So it's allowed. So a rectangle can indeed cover cells from both left and right region across different rows, as long as it doesn't intersect any uncovered square.\n\nThus the solution's claim that each tile belongs wholly to left region or wholly to right region is false. This is a critical error in reasoning because it incorrectly restricts the possible tilings and thus may affect the lower bound. However, we need to check if the lower bound still holds even if we allow such mixed rectangles. The lower bound derived is that at least n-1 rectangles needed to cover left region and at least n-1 needed to cover right region, but if a rectangle can cover both left and right region cells, then perhaps we can reduce the total number of rectangles below 2n-2. Let's see if we can find a tiling with fewer than 2n-2 rectangles. The solution's construction uses 2n-2 rectangles. Could there be a tiling with fewer? Let's try to think: For n=2, minimal number? Let's test small n to see pattern. For n=2, we have a 2x2 grid. Each row and column must have exactly one uncovered square. So uncovered squares are a permutation: either (1,1) and (2,2) or (1,2) and (2,1). Let's try to tile the remaining squares (3 squares) with rectangles. Since each rectangle must be axis-aligned and cannot cover uncovered squares. For n=2, we have 4 squares, 2 uncovered, leaving 2 squares to cover. Actually n^2 - n = 2 squares left uncovered? Wait n=2: n^2=4, uncovered squares = n=2, so covered squares = 2. We need to cover those 2 squares with rectangles. Each rectangle can be size 1x1 (unit square) or larger. We can cover each of the two squares with a 1x1 rectangle each, total 2 rectangles. Could we cover both squares with a single rectangle? The two squares are not adjacent unless they are in same row or column. For permutation (1,1) and (2,2), the uncovered squares are diagonal. The remaining squares are (1,2) and (2,1). They are not adjacent (they are in different rows and columns). A rectangle covering both would be the whole 2x2 grid, which includes uncovered squares, not allowed. So we need at least 2 rectangles. So minimal number for n=2 is 2 = 2n-2 = 2. So matches.\n\nFor n=3, we have 3x3 grid, uncovered squares = 3, covered squares = 6. The solution says minimal = 2n-2 = 4. Let's test if we can do with fewer than 4 rectangles. Could we cover 6 squares with 3 rectangles? Possibly if we have one rectangle covering 4 squares and two covering 1 each, or three covering 2 each. Let's try to find a tiling with 3 rectangles. Suppose we use permutation identity (1,1), (2,2), (3,3). Then left region cells: below diagonal (i>j) are (2,1), (3,1), (3,2) (3 cells). Right region cells: above diagonal (i<j) are (1,2), (1,3), (2,3) (3 cells). The solution uses vertical rectangles covering each column below diagonal: column 1: rows 2-3 (2 cells), column 2: row 3 (1 cell). That's 2 rectangles for left region. Right region: horizontal rectangles covering each row to the right: row 1: columns 2-3 (2 cells), row 2: column 3 (1 cell). That's 2 rectangles for right region. Total 4.\n\nCould we combine some left and right region cells into a single rectangle? For example, rectangle covering cells (2,1) and (2,3) would be row 2, columns 1-3, but includes (2,2) which is uncovered, not allowed. So can't combine across uncovered squares in same row. Could we combine (2,1) and (1,2)? That's rows 1-2, columns 1-2, includes (1,1) and (2,2) uncovered squares, not allowed. Could we combine (2,1) and (3,2)? That's rows 2-3, columns 1-2, includes (2,2) uncovered, not allowed. So any rectangle spanning multiple rows and columns will likely include uncovered squares unless the rectangle is wholly left of all uncovered squares in those rows or wholly right. But we could consider rectangle covering (3,1) and (3,2) (row 3, columns 1-2) includes (3,3) uncovered? No, (3,3) is column 3, not included. So rectangle covering (3,1) and (3,2) is allowed: it's left region for row 3. That's part of the vertical rectangle covering column 1 rows 2-3 plus column 2 row 3? Actually vertical rectangle for column 1 covers (2,1) and (3,1). Horizontal rectangle for row 3 covering right region would be columns 4-? Not relevant. So we could combine (3,1) and (3,2) into a rectangle of size 1x2 (row 3, columns 1-2). That would be left region for row 3 (since sigma(3)=3). So we could cover left region cells with fewer rectangles: for left region, we have cells (2,1), (3,1), (3,2). We could cover (2,1) and (3,1) with vertical rectangle column 1 rows 2-3 (size 2x1). Then we could cover (3,2) with a 1x1 rectangle. That's still 2 rectangles for left region. Could we cover all three left region cells with a single rectangle? That would need to include rows 2-3 and columns 1-2, which includes (2,2) uncovered, not allowed. So cannot.\n\nSimilarly for right region cells (1,2), (1,3), (2,3). Could we cover (1,2) and (1,3) with a horizontal rectangle row 1 columns 2-3 (size 1x2). That's allowed (right region for row 1). Then (2,3) is 1x1 rectangle. So total 2 rectangles for right region. So total 4. So minimal seems 4.\n\nThus for n=3, 2n-2 = 4 seems minimal. For n=4, 2n-2 = 6. Could we do with 5? Possibly? Let's test small n to see pattern. For n=4, uncovered squares are diagonal (1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(4,4). Left region cells: below diagonal: (2,1),(3,1),(3,2),(4,1),(4,2),(4,3) (6 cells). Right region cells: above diagonal: (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,3),(2,4),(3,4) (6 cells). The solution uses vertical rectangles for each column left of diagonal: column 1 rows 2-4 (3 cells), column 2 rows 3-4 (2 cells), column 3 row 4 (1 cell) => 3 rectangles left. Horizontal rectangles for each row right of diagonal: row 1 columns 2-4 (3 cells), row 2 columns 3-4 (2 cells), row 3 column 4 (1 cell) => 3 rectangles right. Total 6.\n\nCould we combine some left and right region cells into fewer rectangles? For left region, we have a shape of a lower triangular region. Could we tile that region with fewer than 3 rectangles? The shape is a Young diagram shape: rows lengths: row 2:1, row3:2, row4:3. This shape can be tiled with rectangles. The minimal number of rectangles needed to tile a Young diagram shape is known to be the number of rows (or columns) maybe? Let's think: The shape is a Ferrers diagram of partition (3,2,1). The minimal number of rectangles to tile a Ferrers diagram is equal to the number of rows (or columns) because each rectangle can be a row of the diagram? Actually we can tile the shape with 3 rectangles: one covering row 4 columns 1-3, one covering row 3 columns 1-2, one covering row 2 column 1. That's exactly the vertical rectangles described. Could we tile with 2 rectangles? Let's try: rectangle covering rows 3-4 columns 1-2 would be a 2x2 block covering cells (3,1),(3,2),(4,1),(4,2). Then we need to cover (2,1) and (4,3). Could we cover (2,1) and (4,3) with a rectangle? No, because they are not aligned. So need at least 3 rectangles. So left region needs at least 3 rectangles. Similarly right region needs at least 3 rectangles. So total at least 6. So minimal is 6.\n\nThus for identity permutation, 2n-2 seems minimal. However, could there be a different permutation that allows mixing left and right region cells in a rectangle, reducing total number? Let's explore. Suppose we choose a permutation that is not monotonic, e.g., sigma = (2,1,4,3,...). Then left region cells and right region cells are interleaved. Could we find a tiling that uses fewer than 2n-2 rectangles? Let's try small n=3 with a non-identity permutation: sigma = (2,3,1). Then uncovered squares: (1,2),(2,3),(3,1). Let's draw grid:\n\nRows 1-3, columns 1-3.\n\nUncovered squares: (1,2) (row1 col2), (2,3) (row2 col3), (3,1) (row3 col1). The remaining cells: all other 6 cells.\n\nLet's list left region cells: For each row i, left region is cells with column < sigma(i). So:\n- Row1: sigma(1)=2, left region: column 1 => cell (1,1)\n- Row2: sigma(2)=3, left region: columns 1-2 => cells (2,1),(2,2)\n- Row3: sigma(3)=1, left region: none (since sigma(3)=1, no column <1)\nRight region cells: columns > sigma(i):\n- Row1: columns >2 => column 3 => cell (1,3)\n- Row2: columns >3 => none\n- Row3: columns >1 => columns 2-3 => cells (3,2),(3,3)\n\nSo left region cells: (1,1),(2,1),(2,2). Right region cells: (1,3),(3,2),(3,3). So total 6 cells.\n\nNow can we tile these 6 cells with fewer than 4 rectangles? Let's try to see if we can combine left and right region cells in a rectangle. For example, rectangle covering rows 1-2, columns 1-2 includes cells (1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2). But (1,2) is uncovered, so not allowed. So can't include column 2 for row1.\n\nWhat about rectangle covering rows 2-3, columns 2-3? That includes (2,2),(2,3),(3,2),(3,3). (2,3) is uncovered (row2 col3). So not allowed.\n\nWhat about rectangle covering rows 1-3, column 1? That includes (1,1),(2,1),(3,1). (3,1) is uncovered, so not allowed.\n\nWhat about rectangle covering rows 1-3, column 3? Includes (1,3),(2,3),(3,3). (2,3) is uncovered, not allowed.\n\nWhat about rectangle covering rows 3, columns 2-3: (3,2),(3,3) allowed (right region). That's a 1x2 rectangle.\n\nWhat about rectangle covering rows 2, columns 1-2: (2,1),(2,2) allowed (left region). That's a 1x2 rectangle.\n\nWhat about rectangle covering rows 1, column 1: (1,1) allowed.\n\nWhat about rectangle covering rows 1, column 3: (1,3) allowed.\n\nThus we have 4 rectangles: (2,1)-(2,2) horizontal, (3,2)-(3,3) horizontal, (1,1) single, (1,3) single. That's 4 rectangles, same as 2n-2 = 4.\n\nCould we combine (1,1) and (1,3) into a rectangle? That would be row 1 columns 1-3 includes (1,2) uncovered, not allowed.\n\nCould we combine (1,1) with (2,1) into a vertical rectangle rows 1-2 column 1 includes (1,1),(2,1) allowed? Yes, (1,1) and (2,1) are both left region cells. So we could have rectangle covering rows 1-2 column 1: (1,1),(2,1). Then we still need (2,2),(1,3),(3,2),(3,3). Could we combine (2,2) and (3,2) into vertical rectangle rows 2-3 column 2? That includes (2,2),(3,2). (2,2) is left region, (3,2) is right region. Is that allowed? Let's check: column 2, rows 2-3. For row2, sigma(2)=3, column 2 < sigma(2), so (2,2) is left region. For row3, sigma(3)=1, column 2 > sigma(3), so (3,2) is right region. The rectangle includes column 2 for both rows, but does it include any uncovered squares? The uncovered squares are (1,2),(2,3),(3,1). Column 2 for row2 is (2,2) not uncovered; column 2 for row3 is (3,2) not uncovered. So rectangle covering (2,2) and (3,2) is allowed! So we can combine left and right region cells in a rectangle! This reduces rectangle count.\n\nNow we have rectangles: vertical rectangle covering (1,1),(2,1); vertical rectangle covering (2,2),(3,2); horizontal rectangle covering (3,3) maybe combine (3,3) with (1,3)? They are not same column or row. Could we combine (1,3) and (3,3) into vertical rectangle rows 1-3 column 3? That includes (2,3) which is uncovered, not allowed. Could we combine (1,3) and (3,3) into something else? No. So we still need (1,3) and (3,3) as separate 1x1 rectangles. So total rectangles: (1,1)-(2,1) vertical, (2,2)-(3,2) vertical, (1,3) single, (3,3) single. That's 4 rectangles again. So we didn't reduce count.\n\nBut maybe we could combine (1,3) and (3,3) with something else? Could we combine (1,3) with (2,2) maybe? That would be rectangle rows 1-2 columns 2-3 includes (1,2) uncovered, not allowed.\n\nThus for n=3, minimal seems 4.\n\nNow for larger n, maybe we can combine left and right region cells in a rectangle to reduce total count below 2n-2. Let's think about general structure. The uncovered squares are a permutation sigma. The left region cells are those (i,j) with j < sigma(i). The right region cells are those with j > sigma(i). The shape of left region is a Young diagram shape defined by sigma (the shape of cells below the permutation graph). The right region is the complement above the permutation graph. The problem reduces to covering the cells of the grid except the permutation cells with axis-aligned rectangles that avoid the permutation cells. This is reminiscent of covering a Ferrers board with rectangles that avoid a set of points (the permutation). The minimal number of rectangles needed to tile the board without covering the points is known to be 2n-2? Let's see if known results exist. This is similar to covering a permutation matrix with monotone rectangles? Might be known as the \"grid covering problem\" or \"minimum number of rectangles to cover all cells except a permutation matrix\". The answer 2n-2 is plausible.\n\nBut we need to verify the solution's reasoning: they claim each tile belongs wholly to left region or wholly to right region. As we saw, a tile could be left of some uncovered squares and right of others, as long as its column interval lies between sigma values of those rows. However, the solution's lower bound argument counts intervals in each column for left region and right region separately, and then says each rectangle can contribute at most one interval to a given column. But if a rectangle covers both left and right region cells in the same column (i.e., for some rows left region, for other rows right region), then it would cover cells in that column that belong to both left and right region. The interval decomposition for left region in column c is the set of rows i such that sigma(i) > c (i.e., uncovered square to the right). For right region, the set of rows i such that sigma(i) < c (uncovered square to the left). The rectangle covering column c for rows [r1, r2] would intersect both sets if sigma values cross c within that row interval. So the rectangle would cover two intervals: one interval of rows where sigma(i) > c (left region) and one interval where sigma(i) < c (right region). So a rectangle could contribute to both left and right region intervals for the same column, thus potentially reducing the total number of rectangles needed. However, the solution's lower bound counts intervals for left region across all columns and intervals for right region across all columns, and then says each rectangle can contribute at most one interval to a given column. Actually they said: \"Each rectangle can contribute at most one interval to a given column, so (9) implies that at least n-1 rectangles are necessary to cover all cells of the left region.\" The statement \"Each rectangle can contribute at most one interval to a given column\" is true for left region intervals: a rectangle can cover at most one contiguous block of rows in column c that are left region cells. But a rectangle could also cover right region intervals in the same column, but that would be counted separately for the right region. So the lower bound for left region is fine: at least n-1 rectangles needed to cover left region cells, regardless of mixing with right region cells. Similarly, at least n-1 rectangles needed to cover right region cells. However, a single rectangle could cover both a left region interval and a right region interval (in possibly different columns). But the lower bound for total number of rectangles is not simply sum of lower bounds for left and right region, because a rectangle could serve both purposes. The solution's claim that \"Hence every tile belongs wholly to the left region or wholly to the right region\" is used to sum the lower bounds. If that claim is false, the lower bound of 2n-2 may not hold.\n\nThus the solution's reasoning is flawed at Level 2: they used an invalid principle (the claim that each tile belongs wholly to left or right region). This is a false statement, not just a missing detail. It's a logical error that invalidates the lower bound. So the reasoning is invalid. However, we need to check if the lower bound could still hold even if some rectangles can be mixed. The solution's lower bound may still be correct, but the justification is flawed. We need to see if the lower bound is indeed correct. If it's correct, then the reasoning is still valid if we can find a valid proof. But the solution's proof is invalid because it uses an incorrect claim. According to the hierarchical decision tree, we need to check if the reasoning uses valid mathematical principles. The claim \"A tile cannot contain a cell from the left region of a certain row and a cell from the right region of the same row. Hence every tile belongs wholly to the left region or wholly to the right region.\" The first part is correct, but the conclusion \"Hence every tile belongs wholly to the left region or wholly to the ri